## 3 Ways to Feed Puppeteer Data into Nuclei## 1. The Endpoint Harvester (Scraping SPAs for Nuclei Fuzzing)
Modern React or Angular websites load thousands of hidden API endpoints via background JavaScript requests. Standard web crawlers can't see them because they don't execute JavaScript.
You can use Puppeteer to click around the site, intercept every background fetch request, save those URLs to a text file, and feed them straight to Nuclei. [6, 7, 8] 

* Step 1: Puppeteer gathers URLs (gather.js)

import puppeteer from 'puppeteer';import fs from 'fs';
const browser = await puppeteer.launch({ executablePath: '/usr/bin/chromium-browser', args: ['--no-sandbox'] });const page = await browser.newPage();const urls = new Set();
// Listen and capture every internal API request made by JavaScript
page.on('request', request => {
  const url = request.url();
  if (url.includes('/api/')) {
    urls.add(url);
  }
});
await page.goto('https://target.com', { waitUntil: 'networkidle2' });
// Save endpoints to a file
fs.writeFileSync('endpoints.txt', Array.from(urls).join('\n'));await browser.close();

* Step 2: Run Nuclei on those specific endpoints

node gather.js
nuclei -list endpoints.txt -tags cve,lfi,sqli,ssrf

